R/helpers.R and inst/template_code/helpers.R include code by Lukas Wallrich
from timesaveR, retained under metaUI's GPL-compatible terms.
The bundled Barroso et al. (2021) dataset was extracted by psymetadata:
https://github.com/josue-rodriguez/psymetadata (GPL-3).
Barroso et al., Psychological Bulletin 147(2), 134–168; DOI 10.1037/bul0000307.
Dataset yi/vi are Fisher-z effects and z-scale sampling variances.
The synthetic inst/examples/tiny.csv fixture is original GPL-3-or-later data;
it is not an empirical benchmark and does not reproduce a paper.
Favicon SVG: original metaUI forest-plot artwork, GPL-3-or-later.

Bundled aksayli2019.csv: psymetadata, Hedges g/variance; Aksayli et al.
(2019), Cogmed working-memory training meta-analysis; https://osf.io/jhavp/.
Bundled coles2019.csv: psymetadata, Cohen d/variance; Coles et al. (2019),
facial-feedback meta-analysis; https://osf.io/v8kxb/.
Source metadata: https://github.com/josue-rodriguez/psymetadata/blob/master/R/data.R
Source-deposit audit on 6 October 2026 via public OSF API:
aksayli2019 source https://osf.io/jhavp/ is CC-BY-4.0 (node year 2018).
Aksayli NM, Sala G, Gobet F (2019), The cognitive and academic benefits of
Cogmed: A meta-analysis. DOI https://doi.org/10.1016/j.edurev.2019.04.003 .
Changes: numeric effects/variances and coded covariates exported through psymetadata.
coles2019 source https://osf.io/v8kxb/ is MIT; node copyright Nicholas A. Coles (2018).
Copyright (c) 2018 Nicholas A. Coles. The complete MIT terms above also apply
with this copyright holder to the Coles source adaptation.
Coles NA, Larsen JT, Lench HC (2019), A meta-analysis of the facial feedback
literature. DOI https://doi.org/10.1037/bul0000194 .
Changes: numeric effects/variances, bibliographic and coded covariates exported
through psymetadata; no article prose or PDFs redistributed.
Barroso's psymetadata source pointer https://osf.io/5admx/ returns 404 from the
public API; its standalone redistribution terms remain unresolved. Its existing
GPL-3 psymetadata attribution is retained; this unresolved source gate prevents
claiming the complete package release-ready.
P-curve calculations originate with Uri Simonsohn, Leif D. Nelson, and Joseph
P. Simmons (2014), through dmetar's implementation. https://www.p-curve.com/
Upstream dmetar DESCRIPTION specifies MIT + file LICENSE; LICENSE identifies
Mathias Harrer (2020). This notice preserves those terms and attribution.

Modified by metaUI on 6 October 2026: dmetar p-curve deduplicates identical
noncentrality solves and restores process options; original MIT notice above retained.

inst/examples/dannheim/mental-health.csv adapts the mental-health forest plot from:
Dannheim I, Ludwig-Walz H, Kirsch H, Bujard M, Buyken AE, Richardson KM, Kroke A
(2025). Effectiveness of leader-targeted stress management interventions: A
systematic review and meta-analysis. Scand J Work Environ Health 51(4):265–281.
DOI https://doi.org/10.5271/sjweh.4219 ; https://www.sjweh.fi/article/4219
Creative Commons Attribution 4.0: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
Changes: subset to the 12-effect mental-health pool; original signed Hedges g
and rounded CIs retained; SE derived from normal 95% CI widths; membership and
derivation from the read-only ES Meta Benchmarks snapshot of 6 October 2026.
That project's released compilation is CC0-1.0 (LICENSE-DATA); the article
adaptation attribution/licence is retained here. No PDFs or source prose bundled.
Example code is GPL-3-or-later. The example is a reanalysis, not author endorsement.
